A||N||9||mbramble||Headliner Material||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||09-27-2000||06:05 PM||||Anyone know of a mail order source for the material for a dark grey interior??  I have read that JC Whitney's med grey is perfect.  I just called to order it but they say 3-4 WEEKS!!  So, I'm looking for an alternative source.....<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-27-2000||06:25 PM||||Try your local auto top place. I got the exact match material for $12 and the owner even gave me the glue.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-27-2000||08:26 PM||||You can also get the glue and headliner at Pep Boys and other chain parts stores. Only porblem is you have to buy a roll that's 9 feet of material.<P>I'm told you can get the fabric by the yard at many fabric stores but it's a little thinner backing. Thinner backing might not be such a bad thing. Usually the stuff falls down because the backing rots. Thinner backing may be a bit stronger.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||09-27-2000||09:35 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fieroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroparts.com</A>   Tim has some headliner material.  At least when I talked to him at the FOCONE show on Saturday.  I don't know the color selection.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||09-27-2000||09:44 PM||||I did one, and got the fabric at a local fabric store, $7.00 a yard. About (2 1/2) I think it was. They also have the 3M foam spray glue. It goes by the name "Headliner Fabric" they had lots of colors and it was an easy job!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>SO be like NIKE   "JUST DO IT"! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000005||Oreif||09-28-2000||01:58 AM||||I used the headliner kit from "Heads Up" which is available at PepBoys and Auto Zone.<BR>The stuff is twice as thick as the fabric store version and is easy to do. They have 2 different shades of gray plus many other colors.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||09-28-2000||02:00 AM||||Hey, _Dad, one of the websites said DON'T use the spray glue.  Are you sayin' you used it and it worked fine?  Was there any odor, causing you to drive w/ windows down?  How little or how much do you hafta spray on?<P> ||||reg||4
Z||000007||Carrolles||09-28-2000||05:43 PM||||I've used 3M FoamFast74 spray glue on the last 5 headliners I've installed.  No problems so far (4 years on the oldest job).  It sprays on like a spider web and does not wet the fabric.  You spray both surfaces to be mated.  You then have about ten minutes to work the fabric and get out wrinkles before it sets.  Works great.<P>I got both the fabric and the glue at Hancock Fabrics in Birmingham, AL.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-28-2000||05:51 PM||||You have to use spray glue that's made for this job like the stuff Carrol used. If you don't one of 3 things happens. <P>heat kills the glue and the fabric falls.<P>the glue eats the foam and ruins everything.<P>the glue may bleed.<P>Pepboys etc have 2 spray glues from the same company that packages teh fabric. One is made for the headliner and the other says don't use it on headliner. (My local PB has both in the same rack. so watch which one you grab.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||mbramble||09-28-2000||09:33 PM||||Found perfect match at a local automotive upholstery shop.  Cost $20 for 1.5 yards and the spray adhesive that they use for attaching headliners.<P>They also beat the price of upholstery material I was going to order since my wife is going to re-upholster the seats.  Ordering it would have cost around $150, it will cost less then $80 locally.  <P>Thanks for the help....<BR>||||reg||1
